    "notes": [
      "Brackets, exemptions and Tables C-E are from the 2026 Form CT-1040ES (Rev. 01/26), which prints the 2026 Tax Calculation Schedule; every amount is unchanged from the 2025 Form CT-1040 TCS (Rev. 12/25). Connecticut's amounts are statutory, not inflation-indexed.",
      "Connecticut has no standard deduction. The personal exemption (Table A: $15,000 single / $24,000 joint / $19,000 HoH) is encoded exactly as the DRS step table in personalExemptionPhaseout: it drops $1,000 for each $1,000 (or part) of Connecticut AGI above $30,000 / $48,000 / $38,000 and is zero above $44,000 / $71,000 / $56,000 (each step starts just above the printed 'more than' bound).",
      "The 2% rate phase-out add-back (Table C: $25 / $50 / $40 per $5,000 / $5,000 / $4,000 step above AGI $56,500 / $100,500 / $78,500, capped at $250 / $500 / $400) and tax recapture (Table D: steps from AGI $105,000 / $210,000 / $168,000 up to $3,400 / $6,800 / $5,320 above $540,000 / $1,080,000 / $864,000) are encoded row for row in agiAddOnTables, using the tables' 'more than' AGI bounds, and added to the Table B bracket tax (worksheet Lines 4-7).",
      "Personal tax credit (Table E: 75% down to 0%, e.g. 10% for single AGI $33,300-$60,000 and joint AGI $52,000-$96,000; none above $64,500 single / $100,500 joint / $78,500 HoH) is a percentage of Lines 4+5+6. Because that total depends only on AGI here, exemptionCreditPhaseout holds the resulting dollar credit as exact piecewise-linear segments (breaks at every Table A, C and E row bound and at bracket kinks). The engine matches the DRS worksheet to the cent before the worksheet's whole-dollar rounding (verified every $500 from $10,000 to $600,000 AGI plus 20,000 off-grid AGIs, all three statuses).",
      "Not modeled: property tax credit, Connecticut EITC, pension/annuity and Social Security modifications, pass-through entity tax credit and Connecticut AMT."
    ],
